# One thing to note is that I calculated my betas using linear regression, which might have been a short cut. book could have better information about more sophisticated ways of doing the betas.

# momentum weighted expected return;

	- Personally I find this a bit degen, but the momentum weighting is just the cum log return over some look back window minus the average of that same window over the stdev

	- that stdev is currently calcd as such: 
 		
	###
	 # cumulative log return over the lookback window (≈ log of total return)
    momentum_raw = momentum_window.sum(axis=0)

    # standardize momentum cross-sectionally (z-score)
    momentum_mean = momentum_raw.mean()
    momentum_std = momentum_raw.std(ddof=0) 
	### 					which is a little sus

# gamma - recall that in the context, gamma is risk aversion which is basically arbitrary

# quadratic programming portfolio optimization: 
	
	max mu T x  - 1/2 gamma x T V x  
	this is the maximum returns subject to minimum risk at your level of risk tolerance
	> hence "mean variance"

